1
00:00:34,290 --> 00:00:40,250
Once upon a time, there was a railway company that kept using XP.

2
00:00:40,990 --> 00:00:43,990
It's been a year since XP's support expired!

3
00:00:44,750 --> 00:00:45,990
We need to replace it immediately!

4
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:49,770
But it still works, doesn't it?

5
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:51,770
Ah, right. That's our company motto.

6
00:00:52,730 --> 00:00:56,110
Mottainai — fix it and it'll run forever.

7
00:00:57,090 --> 00:00:59,316
But we don't have any money.

8
00:00:59,685 --> 00:01:01,676
We're at our limit here!

9
00:01:02,205 --> 00:01:03,870
*sigh*...

10
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:06,830
Let's just hope nothing happens...

11
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:12,193
Waaah! Akane!

12
00:01:12,313 --> 00:01:13,496
It happened.

13
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:16,560
Report!

14
00:01:17,290 --> 00:01:20,170
We're being flooded with virus infection reports!

15
00:01:20,830 --> 00:01:23,350
PR department, Sales, International Affairs—

16
00:01:23,630 --> 00:01:24,937
Everywhere except our division.

17
00:01:28,163 --> 00:01:29,213
What's the cause?

18
00:01:29,355 --> 00:01:30,430
Someone in PR.

19
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:35,786
They fell for a fake website
that told them their computer was infected,

20
00:01:35,906 --> 00:01:38,450
and installed a fake antivirus program.

21
00:01:40,850 --> 00:01:43,890
Which, of course, was itself the virus.

22
00:01:44,690 --> 00:01:46,265
But with it spreading this much—

23
00:01:46,289 --> 00:01:48,950
Why hasn't our division been infected?

24
00:01:49,990 --> 00:01:52,010
Because we're running Windows 7 and Linux.

25
00:01:52,510 --> 00:01:55,830
Indeed, only the XP machines seem to be affected.

26
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,053
What the—!

27
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:12,610
A hacker prank?

28
00:02:16,530 --> 00:02:17,840
Is the CTC server safe?!

29
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:20,680
If it's compromised, the signal system could—

30
00:02:20,855 --> 00:02:21,900
It's fine.

31
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:24,740
It's so old that the virus doesn't affect it.

32
00:02:25,983 --> 00:02:28,540
20th-century technology, by the looks of it.

33
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:30,446
Fascinating.

34
00:02:34,313 --> 00:02:37,330
Cause a service disruption.

35
00:02:39,143 --> 00:02:40,903
Oi, you got here too fast!

36
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:42,130
We're completely cornered!

37
00:02:42,296 --> 00:02:45,899
Aaargh! Aaargh! Aaargh!

38
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:52,856
...We need to disconnect from the internet.

39
00:02:54,513 --> 00:02:55,786
Let's move, people!

40
00:02:56,353 --> 00:02:57,530
Absolutely not!

41
00:02:58,030 --> 00:02:59,030
CIO!

42
00:02:59,350 --> 00:03:00,690
Do you realize the losses?

43
00:03:01,270 --> 00:03:03,813
If the reservation site goes down—

44
00:03:04,082 --> 00:03:06,613
But this is an emergency!

45
00:03:07,630 --> 00:03:09,830
No. The website stays up.

46
00:03:10,770 --> 00:03:11,770
CIO!

47
00:03:14,410 --> 00:03:16,390
This is pointless.

48
00:03:28,389 --> 00:03:29,396
(Phone ringing)

49
00:03:29,470 --> 00:03:31,073
Hello, this is Hosono.

50
00:03:31,263 --> 00:03:32,866
Wh-What do you think you're doing?!

51
00:03:33,430 --> 00:03:38,496
I am about to completely sever
our connection to the internet.

52
00:03:38,786 --> 00:03:40,466
Are you insane?! Stop!

53
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:42,980
Please pass this message along to the executives.

54
00:03:44,340 --> 00:03:47,959
Mottainai — that mindset will be your downfall.

55
00:03:49,515 --> 00:03:52,170
Internet connection severed.

56
00:03:53,194 --> 00:03:55,493
Gaaah! I'm going to get chewed out for this...

57
00:03:56,081 --> 00:03:58,240
*deflates*

58
00:04:00,553 --> 00:04:02,913
A few months later

59
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:06,650
Sorry about the short notice, but...

60
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:12,750
And so, I was seconded to a subsidiary.

61
00:04:13,130 --> 00:04:15,430
This room all the way in the back is Development Section 1.

62
00:04:20,127 --> 00:04:23,120
What!? You love the sound of a DMH-17 engine!?

63
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:24,980
You get it! You really get it!

64
00:04:28,790 --> 00:04:30,820
Just 'cause you're a subsidiary
don't mean you can slack off!

65
00:04:31,230 --> 00:04:33,260
The whole company's paying for this!

66
00:04:33,890 --> 00:04:36,780
Anyway, it goes live next month! Got it!?

67
00:04:39,530 --> 00:04:41,860
Originally, this was supposed to be an easy project.

68
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:45,248
The plan was simply to customize
a product called Kinoko Workflow

69
00:04:45,633 --> 00:04:49,067
for the General Affairs department
and roll it out.

70
00:04:50,230 --> 00:04:52,400
But midway through the project,

71
00:04:52,700 --> 00:04:55,296
someone who can't read the room
started pushing a "logical" argument,

72
00:04:55,430 --> 00:04:57,963
insisting it be deployed to every department.

73
00:04:58,197 --> 00:04:59,330
Great idea!

74
00:04:59,925 --> 00:05:04,630
And so, the team's morale
plummeted to rock bottom.

75
00:05:04,730 --> 00:05:06,797
Akane, I can't do this anymore.

76
00:05:07,190 --> 00:05:08,880
Almost everyone's out sick.

77
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:10,440
Progress has ground to a halt...

78
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:12,100
That's right.

79
00:05:12,930 --> 00:05:14,360
I gave in to temptation.

80
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:18,880
Lt. Commander!

81
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:23,840
Lt. Commander...!

82
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:27,980
So fast!

83
00:05:28,570 --> 00:05:29,570
A-Amazing!

84
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:31,313
Whoa...

85
00:05:31,913 --> 00:05:33,147
What incredible speed...!

86
00:05:33,860 --> 00:05:35,460
That Guinness record is no joke!

87
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:38,420
Ha, a Guinness record is merely a decoration!

88
00:05:38,920 --> 00:05:40,400
Wow, incredible...

89
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:41,900
It's goto art!

90
00:05:42,413 --> 00:05:44,700
Wh— Coding in machine language!?

91
00:05:45,013 --> 00:05:46,113
Behold!

92
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:49,280
The Low-Maintainability Code Master!

93
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:51,379
Absolutely insane.

94
00:05:51,445 --> 00:05:52,445
W-What's that?

95
00:05:52,660 --> 00:05:54,140
The one who caused that huge incident—

96
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:57,500
And who can make any program crash—

97
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:01,160
The Ultimate Bug Trigger!

98
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:03,200
We can't just sit here!

99
00:06:03,520 --> 00:06:04,520
Let's do this!

100
00:06:05,553 --> 00:06:07,353
Raaaaahhh!

101
00:06:07,453 --> 00:06:08,861
So cool!

102
00:06:08,953 --> 00:06:10,020
Go go go!

103
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:12,386
Electrifying!

104
00:06:14,460 --> 00:06:15,460
Lt. Commander...!

105
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:17,400
And now—

106
00:07:53,510 --> 00:07:54,510
Understand?

107
00:07:56,125 --> 00:07:57,990
What we must aim for is perfection.

108
00:07:58,430 --> 00:08:03,330
I want each and every one of you to be aware
that you are the ones supporting this railway.

109
00:08:04,600 --> 00:08:09,130
Safety and security are the keys
to earning our passengers' trust.

110
00:08:10,130 --> 00:08:13,330
As I've said many times: zero computer virus infections!

111
00:08:14,020 --> 00:08:15,890
Zero information security incidents!

112
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:18,550
That should be a given!

113
00:08:19,690 --> 00:08:21,530
And yet you can't even manage that!

114
00:08:22,340 --> 00:08:25,190
Anyone who gets infected is just as guilty as the hacker!

115
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:30,030
Disciplinary action will be unavoidable, of course.

116
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:34,510
I heard someone in the International Division got demoted.

117
00:08:35,320 --> 00:08:37,510
You'd better be especially careful, Mei...

118
00:08:38,170 --> 00:08:44,386
Unfortunately, that concern is well-founded.
(Katsuragi) I want you all to think about our company's role in the local community.

119
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:48,646
I'm next. That's what I thought.
(Katsuragi) Be aware of your duty as railway employees and devote yourselves to your work.

120
00:08:49,870 --> 00:08:54,130
Awareness — right, I need to be more aware.

121
00:08:56,250 --> 00:08:58,070
Understand?

122
00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:00,130
I won't get infected by viruses anymore!

123
00:09:03,853 --> 00:09:06,500
Uuugh... my computer is acting weird.

124
00:09:08,560 --> 00:09:11,040
(Section Chief Yobe) She just said that and already...

125
00:09:12,740 --> 00:09:15,406
We need to contact the System Division...

126
00:09:15,673 --> 00:09:17,885
We'll get fired — both you and me...

127
00:09:18,539 --> 00:09:20,120
Um, Uyagawa, I'm sorry!

128
00:09:20,460 --> 00:09:23,280
Could you please keep this between us?

129
00:09:24,570 --> 00:09:28,040
Sure, no problem.
Just be more careful from now on, okay?

130
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:34,088
I've removed the virus.

131
00:09:34,712 --> 00:09:36,280
Thank you so much!

132
00:09:38,690 --> 00:09:40,440
Saved by the skin of my teeth...

133
00:09:41,050 --> 00:09:43,100
Director Katsuragi isn't kidding around, you know.

134
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:47,500
You really need to be more aware
and stop causing trouble for other people.

135
00:09:47,870 --> 00:09:49,360
Yes...

136
00:09:50,803 --> 00:09:54,870
Ugh... of all days, today is just not my day...

137
00:09:54,936 --> 00:09:56,136
(Phone ringing)

138
00:09:56,270 --> 00:09:59,403
Sorry, Agaho, can you get the phone?

139
00:10:02,870 --> 00:10:05,136
Thank you for calling.

140
00:10:05,203 --> 00:10:06,885
Kyoki Railway PR Department, Agaho speaking.

141
00:10:06,910 --> 00:10:08,860
(Phone voice) My name is Asagiri Yoshimitsu.

142
00:10:10,170 --> 00:10:13,621
You see, starting a few minutes ago, on our website...

143
00:10:13,645 --> 00:10:17,920
We've been receiving suspicious traffic resembling
a DoS attack from your IP address.

144
00:10:19,911 --> 00:10:21,645
Huh!?

145
00:10:22,480 --> 00:10:23,580
IP...?

146
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:25,130
DoS...koi?

147
00:10:25,963 --> 00:10:27,013
Ah!

148
00:10:31,313 --> 00:10:32,647
It was a prank call!

149
00:10:33,510 --> 00:10:35,330
Ah— hey, wait!?

150
00:10:37,375 --> 00:10:39,310
There sure are a lot of weirdos out there.

151
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:43,270
Well, at least that was a change of pace.

152
00:10:43,333 --> 00:10:45,150
(Colleague) The Kobe TV crew is here.

153
00:10:45,383 --> 00:10:47,500
Coming!

154
00:11:05,490 --> 00:11:06,920
Doors closed, check!

155
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:08,646
Side lights, check!

156
00:11:08,713 --> 00:11:09,913
Platform, check!

157
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:12,763
Departure equivalent — proceed!

158
00:11:40,390 --> 00:11:42,360
Ahh... is it quitting time yet...

159
00:11:44,290 --> 00:11:47,140
There are still about 7 hours until quitting time.

160
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:49,090
(Akane) Ugggh...

161
00:11:51,820 --> 00:11:54,153
You seem to be having fun, Lt. Commander.

162
00:11:54,453 --> 00:11:57,119
(Lt. Commander) I'm developing that system! *smugly*

163
00:11:57,203 --> 00:11:58,750
Oh, actual work?

164
00:11:59,190 --> 00:12:00,190
That's rare.

165
00:12:01,890 --> 00:12:02,958
Observe.

166
00:12:02,982 --> 00:12:06,210
I've just implemented a feature that plays
sound effects with keyboard input.

167
00:12:07,282 --> 00:12:11,682
(Keyboard sound effects) Beep beep beep beep beep...

168
00:12:12,290 --> 00:12:14,830
(Computer) Katsudon bowl has been ordered.

169
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:21,150
Plus, sound effects only play when
Scroll Lock is ON — thoughtful design!

170
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:23,850
Nobody uses Scroll Lock anyway!

171
00:12:27,814 --> 00:12:29,700
Well? Logical, isn't it?

172
00:12:29,780 --> 00:12:32,540
The OS I created!

173
00:12:34,740 --> 00:12:36,180
Please do some actual work.

174
00:12:37,860 --> 00:12:38,860
Huh?

175
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:40,300
Look who's talking!

176
00:12:41,790 --> 00:12:42,923
(Phone ringing)

177
00:12:45,910 --> 00:12:48,510
Hello, this is Hosono from the System Division.

178
00:12:49,390 --> 00:12:51,290
(Phone voice) This is Sasayama from the National Railways Police.

179
00:12:51,550 --> 00:12:52,983
(Phone voice) Thank you for your continued support.

180
00:12:53,067 --> 00:12:54,099
Huh, an outside call!?

181
00:12:54,130 --> 00:12:56,110
Oh, th-thank you for your continued support.

182
00:12:57,230 --> 00:12:58,230
(Phone voice) ...Huh?

183
00:12:58,340 --> 00:12:59,599
(Phone voice) Akane?

184
00:13:00,200 --> 00:13:01,033
Huh?

185
00:13:05,910 --> 00:13:07,330
I knew it was Akane!

186
00:13:08,448 --> 00:13:11,332
Long time no see! It's Sasamin!

187
00:13:11,920 --> 00:13:14,537
I'm in Himeji today!

188
00:13:15,265 --> 00:13:16,331
Heh?

189
00:13:17,761 --> 00:13:19,920
This person went to the same high school
and university as me.

190
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:21,920
(Phone voice) Oh, right, right.

191
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:23,720
(Phone voice) I got a job at National Railways!

192
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:25,613
(Phone voice) Looking forward to working with you!

193
00:13:27,030 --> 00:13:30,063
Uh, excuse me, but what is this regarding?

194
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:31,920
Aww, so cold!

195
00:13:32,975 --> 00:13:34,040
Anyway, the real reason I'm calling.

196
00:13:34,680 --> 00:13:38,180
Just between us, we've been getting nonstop alerts since this morning.

197
00:13:38,550 --> 00:13:43,816
(Phone voice) Your system has been repeatedly sending
large amounts of incomplete data to us.

198
00:13:43,916 --> 00:13:44,974
What!?

199
00:13:45,100 --> 00:13:48,216
(Phone voice) So, take care of it ASAP, okay?

200
00:13:49,067 --> 00:13:52,150
(Phone disconnects)

201
00:13:52,300 --> 00:13:53,383
(Lt. Commander) What happened?

202
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:55,308
It was Sasamin.

203
00:13:55,332 --> 00:13:56,640
Sasamin?

204
00:13:57,760 --> 00:13:59,956
Sasami cutlet?

205
00:14:09,340 --> 00:14:12,901
Goodbye, leaving on time.

206
00:14:17,173 --> 00:14:19,940
Ahh...

207
00:14:23,740 --> 00:14:24,740
Report!

208
00:14:26,680 --> 00:14:27,900
Virus infection reports!

209
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:31,320
...None are flooding in.

210
00:14:33,770 --> 00:14:34,960
In fact, there aren't any at all.

211
00:14:35,380 --> 00:14:37,680
That's because Katsuragi's been going around
threatening everyone.

212
00:14:38,500 --> 00:14:40,240
Saying anyone who gets a virus infection
faces disciplinary action.

213
00:14:41,950 --> 00:14:44,500
...Can't you do something about that, CIO?

214
00:14:44,700 --> 00:14:45,700
I can't!

215
00:14:46,060 --> 00:14:48,740
He used to be a union leader — he's a tough one.

216
00:14:50,140 --> 00:14:51,140
I see.

217
00:14:52,090 --> 00:14:53,500
It's not my fault!

218
00:14:55,400 --> 00:14:56,400
Can't be helped.

219
00:14:56,880 --> 00:14:58,560
Let's organize the information we have.

220
00:14:59,946 --> 00:15:00,995
(Lt. Commander) Right.

221
00:15:01,020 --> 00:15:04,560
(Lt. Commander) The station and fare data transfer
to National Railways keeps terminating abnormally.

222
00:15:05,450 --> 00:15:09,000
(Lt. Commander) According to the logs, it's been happening
every few seconds since around 8 AM.

223
00:15:11,300 --> 00:15:14,416
At 9:35, upon request from National Railways,

224
00:15:14,549 --> 00:15:17,533
we severed the dedicated line five minutes later.

225
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:19,167
(Hiroka) Have you interviewed the relevant departments?

226
00:15:19,540 --> 00:15:20,540
Yes.

227
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:21,720
Nothing wrong here.

228
00:15:22,180 --> 00:15:23,180
Really, really.

229
00:15:23,800 --> 00:15:24,800
Business as usual.

230
00:15:26,220 --> 00:15:27,220
...So they say.

231
00:15:28,700 --> 00:15:30,766
Smells like a cover-up...

232
00:15:31,100 --> 00:15:33,000
What about the other systems?

233
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:34,600
They're sluggish overall.

234
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:36,980
It varies by server, but...

235
00:15:37,829 --> 00:15:40,459
Oh — there's a deadlock.

236
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:41,425
Where?

237
00:15:41,450 --> 00:15:43,320
In the HR system's monthly batch processing.

238
00:15:44,350 --> 00:15:47,220
Looks like the spike in access triggered a latent bug.

239
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:52,180
Data distribution to the integration server
has been stalled since this morning.

240
00:15:53,780 --> 00:15:54,780
HR...

241
00:15:55,060 --> 00:15:56,773
So that's why, this morning...

242
00:15:57,893 --> 00:15:59,093
Urgh...

243
00:16:00,905 --> 00:16:03,501
That was a sad incident...

244
00:16:04,660 --> 00:16:06,720
What about network traffic?

245
00:16:06,780 --> 00:16:09,540
Still compiling the numbers, but it's significantly
above normal.

246
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:12,580
Could it be something like packet looping?

247
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:16,480
I don't think so.

248
00:16:17,130 --> 00:16:19,163
ARP traffic is normal.

249
00:16:19,663 --> 00:16:22,063
Then what's causing the increase?

250
00:16:22,459 --> 00:16:23,667
HTTP.

251
00:16:23,691 --> 00:16:24,899
HTTP?

252
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:29,496
Mostly traffic from internal PCs to internal servers —

253
00:16:29,630 --> 00:16:31,897
is dozens of times higher than normal.

254
00:16:33,020 --> 00:16:36,286
Some of it is leaking externally through the proxy.

255
00:16:36,420 --> 00:16:38,520
Any traces of malware!?

256
00:16:38,600 --> 00:16:42,781
So far, no suspicious processes or files have been found.

257
00:16:43,419 --> 00:16:45,670
That's unsettling.

258
00:16:46,230 --> 00:16:47,360
We have no hard evidence yet.

259
00:16:48,170 --> 00:16:49,900
But better safe than sorry.

260
00:16:51,580 --> 00:16:55,500
Lt. Commander, we're cutting off internet access
from the business LAN.

261
00:16:55,685 --> 00:16:56,719
Yes, ma'am.

262
00:16:56,852 --> 00:16:57,860
Hosono.

263
00:16:57,885 --> 00:16:59,871
Contact Risk Management and the PR Department.

264
00:16:59,895 --> 00:17:01,880
Send a company-wide notification via groupware.

265
00:17:02,160 --> 00:17:03,160
Right.

266
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:05,708
That's all right with you, CIO?

267
00:17:05,831 --> 00:17:07,719
Will the reservation site be affected?

268
00:17:08,110 --> 00:17:09,240
Not at the moment.

269
00:17:09,790 --> 00:17:11,320
However, depending on how things develop...

270
00:17:12,360 --> 00:17:13,560
Not this again...

271
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:23,380
What measures do you have in place against cyberattacks?

272
00:17:24,159 --> 00:17:25,226
Well,

273
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:27,526
first, to ensure that attacks cannot reach us,

274
00:17:27,689 --> 00:17:29,593
the systems that manage train operations

275
00:17:29,760 --> 00:17:32,127
are on an isolated network.

276
00:17:33,280 --> 00:17:35,598
However, as the Stuxnet case demonstrated,

277
00:17:35,622 --> 00:17:37,326
even isolated networks

278
00:17:37,377 --> 00:17:39,840
have been identified as vulnerable to attack.

279
00:17:40,700 --> 00:17:42,800
What if you did come under such an attack?

280
00:17:43,913 --> 00:17:44,915
Well,

281
00:17:44,940 --> 00:17:47,352
passenger safety is our top priority,

282
00:17:47,376 --> 00:17:48,713
and when something happens,

283
00:17:48,780 --> 00:17:51,420
our principle is to stop all trains immediately.

284
00:17:51,907 --> 00:17:53,373
In our company's case,

285
00:17:53,540 --> 00:17:54,706
when a system anomaly is detected,

286
00:17:54,973 --> 00:17:57,440
all signals immediately turn red,

287
00:17:57,706 --> 00:18:02,140
and a radio signal ordering an emergency stop
is transmitted automatically.

288
00:18:02,440 --> 00:18:05,200
A radio signal? What kind, specifically?

289
00:18:06,073 --> 00:18:07,573
Beep beep beep.

290
00:18:07,707 --> 00:18:09,273
Beep beep beep?

291
00:18:09,298 --> 00:18:10,873
Beep beep beep.

292
00:18:17,220 --> 00:18:18,363
Emergency broadcast!

293
00:18:18,387 --> 00:18:20,073
It's coming from this command center!

294
00:18:20,273 --> 00:18:21,707
You've gotta be kidding.

295
00:18:25,240 --> 00:18:26,740
Home signal — proceed!

296
00:18:28,010 --> 00:18:30,160
Passing Higashi-Fukuzumi signal station.

297
00:18:31,890 --> 00:18:33,357
Wait—!

298
00:18:33,423 --> 00:18:36,323
Whaaaaaaat!?
(Emergency radio) Beep beep beep...

299
00:18:36,523 --> 00:18:37,356
Stop!

300
00:18:46,186 --> 00:18:47,790
A system anomaly detection!?

301
00:18:47,857 --> 00:18:49,023
Yes!

302
00:18:49,156 --> 00:18:50,580
Multiple signals passed at danger!

303
00:18:50,880 --> 00:18:52,400
Emergency radio has also been triggered!

304
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:57,360
Main Line — 431M, 441M, 2103D!

305
00:18:58,827 --> 00:19:02,860
Reports are coming in that departure signals
suddenly changed to stop aspect.

306
00:19:03,260 --> 00:19:06,393
Tegarayama Station, Himeji Station,
Kyotetsu-Sonobe Station.

307
00:19:06,740 --> 00:19:08,660
The Sanshinki Line too!

308
00:19:09,180 --> 00:19:11,526
Don't you dare start up that train rescheduling thing!

309
00:19:11,760 --> 00:19:14,460
We're not using anything that heavy!

310
00:19:15,293 --> 00:19:18,540
Whaaaat!? I didn't do anything!?

311
00:19:27,526 --> 00:19:29,426
Excuse me.

312
00:19:29,526 --> 00:19:32,660
It's been stopped for an hour now — what's going on!?

313
00:19:33,270 --> 00:19:36,111
I'm sorry... we don't have any new information...

314
00:19:38,840 --> 00:19:40,850
This is 2103D driver.

315
00:19:41,620 --> 00:19:42,950
Transport Command, come in?

316
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:49,150
Transport Command, this is 2103D driver, come in?

317
00:19:51,330 --> 00:19:53,270
Transport Command, you idiots!

318
00:19:54,030 --> 00:19:55,231
(Train radio) This is Transport Command.

319
00:19:55,256 --> 00:19:56,865
(Train radio) Which driver just called us idiots? Over!

320
00:19:57,070 --> 00:19:58,070
Heh, too easy.

321
00:19:58,710 --> 00:20:02,550
This is 2103D driver.
When will operations resume?

322
00:20:04,360 --> 00:20:07,736
This is Transport Command to 2103D driver.

323
00:20:07,827 --> 00:20:09,459
Due to a transport management system malfunction...

324
00:20:09,627 --> 00:20:11,760
(Train radio) You told me that an hour ago!

325
00:20:11,893 --> 00:20:14,260
(Train radio) What kind of malfunction is it?

326
00:20:14,820 --> 00:20:17,030
What happened, and what's the current status!?

327
00:20:17,790 --> 00:20:18,790
What's going on!?

328
00:20:19,275 --> 00:20:21,290
What the hell is going ooooon!?

329
00:20:22,295 --> 00:20:24,010
Inquiries are overwhelming us.

330
00:20:24,650 --> 00:20:25,650
Like I'd know.

331
00:20:25,830 --> 00:20:28,423
We're the ones who want to know the most!

332
00:20:28,757 --> 00:20:31,523
What the hell is happening? Seriously!

333
00:20:32,090 --> 00:20:34,856
What is Signal & Communications doing, huh!?

334
00:20:35,080 --> 00:20:39,323
We've asked KITS and the various vendors
to investigate...

335
00:20:39,357 --> 00:20:41,557
Hey! KITS's what's-his-face!

336
00:20:42,240 --> 00:20:43,570
It's Uyagawa.

337
00:20:44,500 --> 00:20:46,170
You know something, don't you!

338
00:20:46,370 --> 00:20:49,530
I know you do — you were on the transport management
system upgrade project!

339
00:20:49,930 --> 00:20:52,198
I only do terminal maintenance now,

340
00:20:52,222 --> 00:20:55,189
and besides, nothing has been clearly established—

341
00:20:55,289 --> 00:20:56,770
Don't tell me it's a virus!

342
00:20:57,720 --> 00:21:01,289
The current situation is that we haven't found one either...

343
00:21:01,423 --> 00:21:02,690
Tch! Give me a break!

344
00:21:03,250 --> 00:21:07,710
If it is a virus, I'd face disciplinary action...
no way...

345
00:21:07,910 --> 00:21:09,955
My credit card payments...

346
00:21:09,979 --> 00:21:12,710
My revolving credit payments...

347
00:21:13,320 --> 00:21:15,150
What exactly is the situation here?

348
00:21:15,470 --> 00:21:16,770
Well, um...

349
00:21:20,220 --> 00:21:22,460
It must be a virus — it's my fault!

350
00:21:22,940 --> 00:21:26,780
It's because of me... and if they find out,
it won't just be me!

351
00:21:26,781 --> 00:21:29,673
The section chief could lose their job too.

352
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:32,940
Stop causing trouble for other people.

353
00:21:33,240 --> 00:21:34,980
Oh no, what do I do!

354
00:21:35,180 --> 00:21:36,180
What do I do!

355
00:21:36,423 --> 00:21:38,123
What do I do...

356
00:21:38,957 --> 00:21:40,157
!

357
00:21:41,390 --> 00:21:43,557
Hmm...

358
00:21:43,880 --> 00:21:46,380
(Section Chief Yobe) Stop causing trouble for other people!

359
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:49,080
That's it!

360
00:21:49,440 --> 00:21:51,200
Akane isn't "other people"!

361
00:21:51,880 --> 00:21:54,020
I can just ask Akane for help!

362
00:21:55,140 --> 00:21:56,400
Akaneeee!

363
00:21:57,680 --> 00:21:58,680
Here we go.

364
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:01,959
Akaneeeeeee!

365
00:22:03,060 --> 00:22:06,710
It's all my fault... It's all my fault...

366
00:22:06,810 --> 00:22:08,669
Please calm down.

367
00:22:09,490 --> 00:22:10,490
It's okay.

368
00:22:10,875 --> 00:22:13,560
If you turn yourself in, the sentence will be lighter.

369
00:22:13,693 --> 00:22:17,060
I didn't do anything!

370
00:22:17,410 --> 00:22:20,210
This one's contradicting herself already.

371
00:22:22,590 --> 00:22:25,650
It seems like the transport management system
is having issues too.

372
00:22:26,190 --> 00:22:27,350
All services are suspended.

373
00:22:28,150 --> 00:22:29,150
What!?

374
00:22:29,470 --> 00:22:32,281
That's outside our jurisdiction,
so we don't have much info...

375
00:22:32,982 --> 00:22:34,748
Could it be the same incident?

376
00:22:35,090 --> 00:22:37,590
If so, that narrows down the possibilities.

377
00:22:38,910 --> 00:22:43,350
The transport management network and the
information/business network are separated, right?

378
00:22:44,290 --> 00:22:46,543
The only things connected to both networks

379
00:22:46,657 --> 00:22:51,090
are the authentication server and
the update distribution server for terminals.

380
00:22:51,300 --> 00:22:52,670
Could those be infected with malware?

381
00:22:55,390 --> 00:22:56,970
There's one more possibility.

382
00:22:57,870 --> 00:23:01,751
The possibility that an insider
is deliberately attacking us.

383
00:23:04,030 --> 00:23:06,750
Right... Not something we'd want to hear, but...

384
00:23:19,900 --> 00:23:20,900
Logs?

385
00:23:21,650 --> 00:23:23,180
Retention periods vary.

386
00:23:24,135 --> 00:23:26,040
Some may already be gone.

387
00:23:26,480 --> 00:23:29,520
But there may still be important traces remaining.

388
00:23:30,420 --> 00:23:32,786
Let's gather and preserve the logs together.

389
00:23:33,053 --> 00:23:35,060
CIO Nakafunyu?

390
00:23:35,135 --> 00:23:36,953
O-Okay...

391
00:23:37,153 --> 00:23:39,620
(Hiroka) Report to the president
without Director Katsuragi finding out.

392
00:23:39,753 --> 00:23:41,720
(Hiroka) Make sure it goes smoothly.

393
00:23:41,840 --> 00:23:42,840
What!?

394
00:23:42,980 --> 00:23:43,980
Me!?

395
00:23:47,220 --> 00:23:49,360
O-Okay, fine...

396
00:23:52,025 --> 00:23:55,040
Um, can I go back to my desk?

397
00:23:56,058 --> 00:23:58,792
The atmosphere here is the worst.

398
00:23:59,300 --> 00:24:00,300
Wait.

399
00:24:00,500 --> 00:24:01,540
Hosono, stay there.

400
00:24:02,210 --> 00:24:04,080
Share information with Transport Command.

401
00:24:05,620 --> 00:24:07,091
Ugh...

402
00:24:07,891 --> 00:24:10,800
Listen. A system isn't just computers.

403
00:24:11,300 --> 00:24:13,500
It includes people too.

404
00:24:13,990 --> 00:24:18,600
Properly communicating the situation to users
is also a vital role for an engineer.

405
00:24:21,660 --> 00:24:22,848
Oh, hello.

406
00:24:22,982 --> 00:24:24,915
Huh? Who the hell are you?

407
00:24:25,540 --> 00:24:27,448
I'm Hosono from the PR Department, System Division.

408
00:24:27,715 --> 00:24:29,015
Hah? PR?

409
00:24:29,400 --> 00:24:30,400
Hello.

410
00:24:30,560 --> 00:24:33,748
It's the System Division, freeloading under
the PR Department due to budget constraints.

411
00:24:34,448 --> 00:24:36,348
Yeah, I know. What do you want?

412
00:24:37,040 --> 00:24:38,462
Sorry for the verbal notice, but

413
00:24:38,487 --> 00:24:40,906
I have something urgent to tell everyone.

414
00:24:48,020 --> 00:24:49,110
—And that's the current situation.

415
00:24:49,815 --> 00:24:53,034
Well, to borrow Director Katsuragi's words,

416
00:24:53,146 --> 00:24:56,568
it's basically a "disciplinary action for every employee"
kind of situation.

417
00:24:56,901 --> 00:24:58,634
Are you sure about this!?

418
00:24:58,830 --> 00:25:02,410
Um, you're scaring me, could you lower your tone?

419
00:25:02,850 --> 00:25:04,310
...Are you sure about this?

420
00:25:05,045 --> 00:25:06,270
We can't confirm it yet.

421
00:25:07,150 --> 00:25:10,338
However, considering what's actually happening,

422
00:25:10,362 --> 00:25:14,516
I believe we need to act
on the safest possible assumption.

423
00:25:14,583 --> 00:25:16,450
Damn... This is terrible...

424
00:25:17,330 --> 00:25:19,206
A cyberattack is suspected.

425
00:25:19,273 --> 00:25:21,790
The transport management system cannot be trusted.

426
00:25:22,290 --> 00:25:23,762
Is that a fair assessment?

427
00:25:23,806 --> 00:25:24,829
...Yes.

428
00:25:24,854 --> 00:25:26,207
How long have you known?

429
00:25:26,350 --> 00:25:28,740
Since around 9 AM.

430
00:25:28,765 --> 00:25:30,873
Why didn't you say something sooner!?

431
00:25:31,110 --> 00:25:34,870
Thorough communication! Accident prevention!
Compliance with regulations!

432
00:25:35,590 --> 00:25:36,690
It's on the back of your badge!

433
00:25:37,590 --> 00:25:38,590
I'm sorry.

434
00:25:40,410 --> 00:25:44,134
Wait. Whose fault was it
that safety wasn't maintained?

435
00:25:44,268 --> 00:25:46,379
Why did it come to this?

436
00:25:52,550 --> 00:25:54,650
It's all my fault, isn't it?

437
00:25:55,410 --> 00:25:56,410
Hey, Yabase.

438
00:25:57,560 --> 00:26:00,365
You held back the report because
you were afraid of me, right?

439
00:26:00,515 --> 00:26:05,148
Uh, well, um...

440
00:26:06,170 --> 00:26:08,198
That's part of it...

441
00:26:08,648 --> 00:26:11,882
But Director Katsuragi...

442
00:26:14,210 --> 00:26:15,230
...I'm sorry.

443
00:26:15,590 --> 00:26:16,590
It was my fault.

444
00:26:17,510 --> 00:26:20,240
Thank you for being honest.

445
00:26:20,607 --> 00:26:22,323
Everyone, listen up!

446
00:26:22,580 --> 00:26:24,070
It is now 11:05 AM.

447
00:26:24,490 --> 00:26:28,090
As of this moment, we are treating this
as a critical incident.

448
00:26:30,080 --> 00:26:31,590
Accidents will happen. That's inevitable.

449
00:26:31,970 --> 00:26:32,970
That's just reality.

450
00:26:33,720 --> 00:26:35,750
Zero liability accidents. Zero incidents.

451
00:26:36,050 --> 00:26:37,350
Those are just targets.

452
00:26:38,010 --> 00:26:40,730
A zero achieved through cover-ups
or word games is meaningless.

453
00:26:41,330 --> 00:26:42,993
What matters most right now

454
00:26:43,127 --> 00:26:45,560
is getting our passengers to their destinations safely.

455
00:26:47,150 --> 00:26:50,130
I'm the one who caused this situation.
It's my responsibility.

456
00:26:51,540 --> 00:26:53,890
I played favorites and built walls between us.

457
00:26:54,825 --> 00:26:56,770
I consider that itself an incident.

458
00:26:58,490 --> 00:27:02,710
Realizing this only now is shameful
for someone in the railway industry.

459
00:27:03,510 --> 00:27:07,850
But safety can only be achieved
with everyone's cooperation.

460
00:27:08,660 --> 00:27:10,923
So please, lend me your strength.

461
00:27:11,257 --> 00:27:12,923
I'm begging you.

462
00:27:16,110 --> 00:27:18,510
At that moment, everyone remembered.

463
00:27:20,140 --> 00:27:22,290
General Rules, Accident Prevention:

464
00:27:22,865 --> 00:27:26,544
"All employees shall cooperate as one
in the prevention of accidents,

465
00:27:26,728 --> 00:27:30,377
and shall ensure that no harm comes
to passengers or the public,

466
00:27:30,402 --> 00:27:33,411
giving their utmost effort to this end."

467
00:27:33,580 --> 00:27:36,470
Today is the day to show what
our training has prepared us for.

468
00:27:38,120 --> 00:27:41,690
Everyone, follow the emergency procedures manual.

469
00:27:41,850 --> 00:27:45,770
If there's any unexpected situation or sign of danger,
contact me immediately.

470
00:27:48,025 --> 00:27:49,410
False alarms are fine.

471
00:27:49,632 --> 00:27:51,915
Anyone who prevents an accident before it happens,

472
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:53,998
anyone who contributes to damage reduction—

473
00:27:54,023 --> 00:27:59,981
no matter what Katsuragi says,
I will officially commend them under my authority.

474
00:28:00,165 --> 00:28:01,448
Understood!?

475
00:28:01,770 --> 00:28:02,770
Yes, sir!

476
00:28:03,930 --> 00:28:04,930
Hey, Tsuruga.

477
00:28:05,210 --> 00:28:07,530
I'm appointing you as incident recorder.

478
00:28:08,170 --> 00:28:09,170
Begin recording.

479
00:28:09,370 --> 00:28:13,950
Understood, beginning recording.
What about video documentation?

480
00:28:14,930 --> 00:28:16,370
There happens to be a camera over there.

481
00:28:17,270 --> 00:28:18,490
Request their cooperation.

482
00:28:19,373 --> 00:28:20,940
What!?

483
00:28:21,970 --> 00:28:23,456
Hey, Yamaguchi.

484
00:28:23,840 --> 00:28:30,298
Per the manual, make a preliminary phone report to
the Risk Management Committee and
the Kinki Transport Bureau's Railway Safety Division.

485
00:28:31,390 --> 00:28:34,830
The Transport Bureau — it'll be the usual guy
answering the phone.

486
00:28:35,150 --> 00:28:37,890
He asks a lot of questions,
so have your answers ready.

487
00:28:38,310 --> 00:28:43,850
Roger. By the way, is this classified as
a railway operations accident or a service disruption?

488
00:28:44,570 --> 00:28:46,150
Too unprecedented to say.

489
00:28:47,150 --> 00:28:52,810
"Disrupts mainline operations for over three hours"
— wouldn't it be both?

490
00:28:53,723 --> 00:28:56,823
Then for our reporting basis,

491
00:28:57,023 --> 00:29:04,030
we'll cite the Railway Accident Reporting Rules,
Article 5, Paragraph 1, Item 5, and Paragraph 2, Item 1.

492
00:29:04,600 --> 00:29:06,290
There's also a suspected cyberattack.

493
00:29:06,670 --> 00:29:09,650
Add "deemed particularly exceptional" as well.

494
00:29:11,020 --> 00:29:12,998
Root cause analysis can wait.

495
00:29:13,165 --> 00:29:15,248
How many hours to restore?

496
00:29:15,410 --> 00:29:16,981
I don't know.

497
00:29:17,270 --> 00:29:21,448
We can't even begin restoration
without first identifying the virus.

498
00:29:22,590 --> 00:29:26,390
Worst case, we'll have to set up
the entire system from scratch.

499
00:29:27,150 --> 00:29:31,273
In that case, the earliest we could restore
would be tomorrow's first train.

500
00:29:31,640 --> 00:29:33,673
If it takes longer, a full week...

501
00:29:34,091 --> 00:29:35,965
Understood.

502
00:29:36,098 --> 00:29:38,215
Suspended until tomorrow morning!?

503
00:29:38,470 --> 00:29:40,031
Like hell. Don't be stupid.

504
00:29:40,215 --> 00:29:42,032
Substitute block signaling.

505
00:29:42,098 --> 00:29:44,510
Substitute block signaling!? On the entire line!?

506
00:29:45,470 --> 00:29:46,965
Seriously...

507
00:29:47,165 --> 00:29:48,632
(Managura) Dead serious.

508
00:29:48,830 --> 00:29:49,830
Five hours.

509
00:29:50,360 --> 00:29:51,910
I'll buy you five hours.

510
00:29:52,530 --> 00:29:53,530
That's the limit.

511
00:29:54,140 --> 00:29:58,623
Get at least the automatic block signaling
and CTC back before the morning rush.

512
00:30:00,489 --> 00:30:04,170
Nnngh!

513
00:30:08,039 --> 00:30:10,106
Meow~

514
00:30:12,570 --> 00:30:14,065
Stationmaster Tsukita!

515
00:30:15,440 --> 00:30:16,440
Are you all right!?

516
00:30:17,350 --> 00:30:19,580
We're resuming operations using the pilot communication system.

517
00:30:23,110 --> 00:30:27,580
This is Transport Command.
Attention all trains operating within our territory.

518
00:30:28,300 --> 00:30:34,160
With the restoration of automatic block signals,
we will be switching back from substitute block
to automatic block sequentially—

519
00:30:35,177 --> 00:30:38,467
The signals are fixed, so why are we stopped again?

520
00:30:39,030 --> 00:30:44,040
Only the signals between stations have been restored.
The station signals are still down.

521
00:30:48,430 --> 00:30:53,073
If only the train on the opposite platform
would depart...

522
00:30:53,273 --> 00:30:56,390
But they're fumbling with manual procedures
over there...

523
00:30:58,440 --> 00:31:01,100
Th-Th-This is bad, driver!

524
00:31:01,600 --> 00:31:06,140
The following train already caught up to us!

525
00:31:06,141 --> 00:31:07,141
What!?

526
00:31:12,660 --> 00:31:15,720
Transport Commaaaaand!

527
00:31:17,300 --> 00:31:18,300
A deadlock?

528
00:31:19,335 --> 00:31:20,540
Yes, I'm sorry.

529
00:31:21,122 --> 00:31:23,123
During the block system changeover,

530
00:31:23,389 --> 00:31:27,823
a misidentification of train positions
led to incorrect departure orders,

531
00:31:27,848 --> 00:31:30,773
resulting in trains unable to move.

532
00:31:31,105 --> 00:31:35,440
If at least the train position display
had been restored, this wouldn't have happened...

533
00:31:36,560 --> 00:31:38,360
It's a deadlock!

534
00:31:38,460 --> 00:31:39,460
Hm?

535
00:31:40,110 --> 00:31:42,160
A deadlock!? At which station?

536
00:31:42,620 --> 00:31:44,960
No, I mean inside the system.

537
00:31:46,150 --> 00:31:48,498
According to the system developer,

538
00:31:48,632 --> 00:31:52,982
it seems a deadlock is occurring
in the operations rescheduling module.

539
00:31:54,160 --> 00:31:57,140
Why would a deadlock happen inside the system?

540
00:31:57,880 --> 00:32:00,020
Deadlocks are a train thing.

541
00:32:01,120 --> 00:32:02,140
Well actually,

542
00:32:02,800 --> 00:32:04,560
they happen inside systems too.

543
00:32:05,773 --> 00:32:08,123
To prevent collisions, trains

544
00:32:08,215 --> 00:32:12,239
are controlled by signals so only one train
can be in a section at a time, right?

545
00:32:12,625 --> 00:32:14,120
Yeah, that's block signaling.

546
00:32:15,680 --> 00:32:19,260
What happens if a dispatch error puts trains
going opposite directions together?

547
00:32:19,870 --> 00:32:22,520
They block each other's path and can't move.

548
00:32:23,010 --> 00:32:24,200
That's a deadlock.

549
00:32:25,640 --> 00:32:28,090
In fact, data inside a system

550
00:32:28,140 --> 00:32:33,995
is also controlled so only one person can edit it
at a time, to prevent data corruption.

551
00:32:34,020 --> 00:32:35,200
Same as block signaling.

552
00:32:36,630 --> 00:32:39,940
And if data processing requests
in different orders get mixed together?

553
00:32:40,340 --> 00:32:42,600
A deadlock occurs.

554
00:32:43,690 --> 00:32:45,168
So this trouble is basically

555
00:32:45,192 --> 00:32:47,888
like a deadlock at a single station

556
00:32:47,912 --> 00:32:50,480
bringing down the entire line?

557
00:32:51,420 --> 00:32:53,090
Yes, most likely.

558
00:32:53,640 --> 00:32:58,900
With this few terminals, it normally shouldn't
be possible to take down the system.

559
00:32:59,330 --> 00:33:01,080
That's what puzzled me until now.

560
00:33:01,830 --> 00:33:04,520
But with a deadlock, even two terminals can do it.

561
00:33:05,360 --> 00:33:10,281
But the operations rescheduling module
is so resource-heavy we banned its use.

562
00:33:10,448 --> 00:33:13,265
Who would be using that thing?

563
00:33:13,630 --> 00:33:17,406
Whether it's a DoS attack or just a bug —
that's the question.

564
00:33:18,073 --> 00:33:19,507
Dos...koi!?

565
00:33:20,100 --> 00:33:24,520
We detected suspicious traffic resembling
a DoS attack from your IP address.

566
00:33:27,380 --> 00:33:29,673
DoS attack.

567
00:33:29,990 --> 00:33:32,223
DoS attack.

568
00:33:32,606 --> 00:33:34,223
Dos...koi?

569
00:33:34,373 --> 00:33:37,273
(Sumo wrestler) DOSUKOI!

570
00:33:41,256 --> 00:33:45,081
Hey, hey, Akane, what's "dos...koi"?

571
00:33:45,215 --> 00:33:47,465
It's a DoS attack.

572
00:33:47,798 --> 00:33:48,932
(Sumo wrestler) DOSUKOI!

573
00:33:49,100 --> 00:33:55,880
Well, in terms of bringing something down
with brute force, that's not entirely off the mark.

574
00:33:56,960 --> 00:34:00,689
Thank you for that... novel contribution.

575
00:34:01,156 --> 00:34:03,023
That's not what I meant!

576
00:34:03,160 --> 00:34:04,160
This morning!

577
00:34:04,660 --> 00:34:08,940
I got a call about dos...koi and IPs and whatnot!

578
00:34:09,448 --> 00:34:12,181
From some "Yoshimitsu" something or other.

579
00:34:12,282 --> 00:34:14,998
From Professor Asagiri Yoshimitsu!?

580
00:34:15,170 --> 00:34:17,100
The security expert!?

581
00:34:17,500 --> 00:34:18,589
...Probably.

582
00:34:18,907 --> 00:34:21,889
I thought it was a prank call, so I hung up.

583
00:34:23,389 --> 00:34:25,660
Ah, pardon the sudden call.

584
00:34:26,180 --> 00:34:30,598
This is Hosono from Kyoki Railway.

585
00:34:30,997 --> 00:34:32,663
Grrrrrrr...

586
00:34:32,896 --> 00:34:36,060
Whoa, he's really angry!

587
00:34:36,960 --> 00:34:39,120
What!? Even external websites!?

588
00:34:39,845 --> 00:34:42,660
Yes, it really does seem to be
an indiscriminate attack.

589
00:34:43,489 --> 00:34:47,156
Hmm, could it actually be a simple mechanism?

590
00:34:47,489 --> 00:34:48,539
(Keyboard SFX) Beep beep beep...

591
00:34:48,564 --> 00:34:49,572
Hm?

592
00:34:49,856 --> 00:34:51,339
Emergency signal!?

593
00:34:53,180 --> 00:34:54,480
Where!?

594
00:34:54,481 --> 00:34:56,496
No, that's just a keyboard sound effect.

595
00:34:56,597 --> 00:34:58,463
(Keyboard SFX) Beep beep beep...

596
00:34:58,647 --> 00:35:00,163
(Managura) Oh, that's confusing.

597
00:35:00,590 --> 00:35:03,140
I was testing my custom OS, and...

598
00:35:04,615 --> 00:35:07,320
Playing around at a time like— Hm?

599
00:35:07,648 --> 00:35:09,365
(Keyboard SFX) Beep beep beep...

600
00:35:09,560 --> 00:35:10,560
(Akane) F5?

601
00:35:10,664 --> 00:35:12,860
(Lt. Commander) F5! The F5 key!

602
00:35:13,440 --> 00:35:16,098
(Lt. Commander) The F5 key is being pressed
repeatedly on its own!?

603
00:35:16,431 --> 00:35:18,780
So this is an "F5 attack"?

604
00:35:20,123 --> 00:35:21,273
If it's the F5 key—

605
00:35:21,490 --> 00:35:23,723
The operations rescheduling module!

606
00:35:24,857 --> 00:35:26,357
That's it!

607
00:35:26,660 --> 00:35:32,140
So the F5 attack triggered
the operations rescheduling module,

608
00:35:33,580 --> 00:35:38,640
which triggered its chronic deadlock bug,
escalating into a system failure. Is that right?

609
00:35:40,326 --> 00:35:44,548
However, to infect my custom OS...
Quite the skilled adversary.

610
00:35:46,414 --> 00:35:48,898
Regardless of the OS—

611
00:35:49,400 --> 00:35:50,720
Could it be!?

612
00:35:50,848 --> 00:35:51,865
(Keyboard SFX) Beep beep beep...

613
00:35:52,048 --> 00:35:53,782
(Keyboard sound stops)

614
00:35:54,895 --> 00:35:57,114
It stopped when I unplugged the keyboard.

615
00:35:57,398 --> 00:35:58,332
(Managura) No way!

616
00:35:59,710 --> 00:36:00,710
Hey, everyone!

617
00:36:00,870 --> 00:36:03,156
Unplug your keyboards!

618
00:36:11,715 --> 00:36:13,615
Ohh!

619
00:36:14,410 --> 00:36:16,960
It was never a virus to begin with.

620
00:36:17,080 --> 00:36:21,448
But didn't that guy remove
a virus this morning?

621
00:36:21,698 --> 00:36:23,565
(Phone vibration)

622
00:36:23,970 --> 00:36:24,970
This is Yumesaki.

623
00:36:25,100 --> 00:36:28,380
Is there anything suspicious connected to Terminal 13?

624
00:36:29,073 --> 00:36:31,580
Terminal 13...

625
00:36:32,073 --> 00:36:33,223
(Uyagawa) Heave-ho...

626
00:36:33,323 --> 00:36:34,390
Ah!

627
00:36:39,949 --> 00:36:40,949
Ow ow ow ow!

628
00:36:41,139 --> 00:36:42,139
Hey!

629
00:36:42,280 --> 00:36:43,280
What are you doing!?

630
00:36:44,030 --> 00:36:45,540
Clever of you.

631
00:36:46,690 --> 00:36:49,389
If you repeatedly press F5 on multiple terminals,

632
00:36:49,939 --> 00:36:52,960
it causes a deadlock in the train operations
management system.

633
00:36:55,040 --> 00:36:55,760
Right?

634
00:36:55,810 --> 00:36:58,781
You were rejected during the Train Operations
Management System renewal project

635
00:36:58,965 --> 00:37:02,348
by the Chief Dispatcher, weren't you, Mr. Uyagawa?

636
00:37:02,847 --> 00:37:04,563
Furthermore,

637
00:37:05,280 --> 00:37:08,313
a small computer was hidden inside the keyboard,

638
00:37:08,513 --> 00:37:13,447
programmed to trigger the F5 barrage only when
the Scroll Lock key was turned on.

639
00:37:15,240 --> 00:37:16,240
Right?

640
00:37:16,960 --> 00:37:21,060
The person who could remove a virus
just by toggling Scroll Lock — Mr. Uyagawa?

641
00:37:23,060 --> 00:37:29,000
These keyboards were purchased from your company,
Kyotetsu IT Solutions.

642
00:37:30,180 --> 00:37:31,180
Right?

643
00:37:31,560 --> 00:37:33,540
A supply chain attack, in a way!

644
00:37:35,565 --> 00:37:36,565
Mr. Uyagawa?

645
00:37:38,960 --> 00:37:40,400
Ha ha ha...

646
00:37:40,450 --> 00:37:42,180
Go ask the keyboard supplier!

647
00:37:42,680 --> 00:37:43,680
It wasn't me!

648
00:37:43,940 --> 00:37:44,940
Is that so?

649
00:37:45,720 --> 00:37:47,665
From what we heard about the Agaho incident,

650
00:37:47,915 --> 00:37:51,615
it seems you knew everything from the start.

651
00:37:51,782 --> 00:37:54,082
Yeah, I knew about the keyboard defect.

652
00:37:54,710 --> 00:37:58,189
Then why, when the operations management system
went down,

653
00:37:58,407 --> 00:38:02,523
didn't you point out the keyboard anomaly?

654
00:38:02,630 --> 00:38:08,580
No — rather, why didn't you propose
replacing the keyboards much sooner?

655
00:38:09,200 --> 00:38:12,500
Well, Mr. Uyagawa, you ninja washout?

656
00:38:13,810 --> 00:38:17,000
But Scroll Lock doesn't turn on by itself!

657
00:38:19,530 --> 00:38:22,700
Indeed, that puzzled me too.

658
00:38:23,460 --> 00:38:27,300
The F5 barrage wouldn't trigger
unless someone turned it on.

659
00:38:28,070 --> 00:38:30,840
(Uyagawa) It was the PR Department's
Ultimate Bug Trigger!

660
00:38:31,690 --> 00:38:36,620
True, at least the incidents in the PR Division
were caused that way.

661
00:38:37,682 --> 00:38:38,848
However—

662
00:38:39,310 --> 00:38:43,848
Mr. Uyagawa, this USB drive you were carrying—

663
00:38:43,998 --> 00:38:47,582
something curious happens when you plug it in.

664
00:38:48,789 --> 00:38:53,165
Despite being a USB drive,
it behaves like a keyboard,

665
00:38:53,298 --> 00:38:57,465
automatically inputting the Scroll Lock key
and turning Scroll Lock on.

666
00:38:57,632 --> 00:38:59,615
Heh heh heh.

667
00:39:00,340 --> 00:39:01,700
And that's not all.

668
00:39:02,536 --> 00:39:05,174
It opens "Run",

669
00:39:05,336 --> 00:39:08,236
and using a combination of PowerShell and psexec,

670
00:39:08,435 --> 00:39:13,904
it turns on Scroll Lock
on every computer in the network.

671
00:39:16,150 --> 00:39:20,300
And it's thoughtfully designed to execute
every few minutes.

672
00:39:20,630 --> 00:39:25,120
Ha, ha ha ha...
Quite an elaborate method, isn't it?

673
00:39:25,500 --> 00:39:28,940
But I saw something suspicious plugged in
and removed it.

674
00:39:29,280 --> 00:39:30,280
That's all.

675
00:39:32,032 --> 00:39:34,615
Oh? Even now, you maintain that?

676
00:39:35,364 --> 00:39:36,798
However,

677
00:39:36,823 --> 00:39:41,882
the moment you plugged in that USB drive
was caught on camera.

678
00:39:41,907 --> 00:39:44,178
By this fine fellow.

679
00:39:45,281 --> 00:39:46,648
Bzzz.

680
00:39:46,732 --> 00:39:49,498
The multi-purpose self-propelled Pachira unit.

681
00:39:50,815 --> 00:39:51,882
Huh!?

682
00:39:56,090 --> 00:39:59,820
What's more, the TV camera also caught you
from another angle.

683
00:40:01,045 --> 00:40:06,700
And you know, psexec does leave traces
in the logs, Mr. Uyagawa.

684
00:40:07,260 --> 00:40:10,100
That's right. There's no escape now.

685
00:40:11,690 --> 00:40:16,781
The culprit is you, Uyagawa Yasu.

686
00:40:16,982 --> 00:40:20,082
Q.E.D.

687
00:40:28,890 --> 00:40:31,790
I get off work in 30 minutes,

688
00:40:31,940 --> 00:40:34,873
so confess already, would you?

689
00:40:35,423 --> 00:40:37,707
Damn it! Damn it!

690
00:40:38,473 --> 00:40:41,823
Yeah, that attitude is what pisses me off!

691
00:40:42,023 --> 00:40:44,620
So what if you're the parent company!?
You're all garbage!

692
00:40:45,375 --> 00:40:48,020
After everything I've done for you,
every last one of you!

693
00:40:49,065 --> 00:40:50,600
That's why I did it.

694
00:40:51,400 --> 00:40:54,145
(Yumesaki) Uyagawa, I understand how you feel, but...

695
00:40:54,169 --> 00:40:57,970
(Akane) Well, they really are garbage, but...
what else is new?

696
00:40:57,994 --> 00:41:01,340
...It's because of your garbage projects
that we got demoted!

697
00:41:02,320 --> 00:41:04,381
Well, anticipating something like this,

698
00:41:04,432 --> 00:41:06,798
I'd been quietly setting this up for three years.

699
00:41:07,448 --> 00:41:09,615
(Katsuragi) What did you say!?

700
00:41:11,170 --> 00:41:13,820
Do you have any idea how much damage
you've caused!?

701
00:41:14,760 --> 00:41:16,440
Ha ha ha ha ha!

702
00:41:16,490 --> 00:41:17,960
You brought this on yourselves!

703
00:41:18,660 --> 00:41:23,100
Your behavior made everyone
start covering things up.

704
00:41:23,935 --> 00:41:25,923
That's why the response was delayed!

705
00:41:26,039 --> 00:41:29,420
Congratulations! You're an "accomplice"
of the hacker!

706
00:41:29,960 --> 00:41:30,960
Ha ha ha ha!

707
00:41:31,080 --> 00:41:32,180
Show me!

708
00:41:32,600 --> 00:41:35,440
That "railroader's pride" of yours!

709
00:41:35,840 --> 00:41:38,020
Ha ha ha ha ha ha ha!

710
00:41:46,690 --> 00:41:49,780
Yesterday, I cleaned up my room.

711
00:41:50,520 --> 00:41:53,020
Oh, that's rare.

712
00:41:54,543 --> 00:41:55,543
Eek!

713
00:42:01,890 --> 00:42:03,390
My ears!

714
00:42:27,840 --> 00:42:30,190
Ah! A U-F-O!

715
00:42:31,020 --> 00:42:33,340
Oh, you're right!

716
00:42:35,270 --> 00:42:37,360
A "U-FO"...nium (euphonium)!?

717
00:42:42,773 --> 00:42:43,606
(Performance)

718
00:42:55,781 --> 00:42:58,081
Wait, this is a tuba!

719
00:42:58,220 --> 00:42:59,620
I thought it was heavy.

720
00:43:00,240 --> 00:43:01,295
Well then!

721
00:43:01,379 --> 00:43:02,445
Bye-bye!

722
00:43:02,800 --> 00:43:03,800
Oh no!

723
00:43:04,350 --> 00:43:06,900
The tsukkomi-bait vulnerability got me...

724
00:43:07,380 --> 00:43:13,589
I was so close... to leaving on time...

725
00:43:13,690 --> 00:43:17,507
There's nothing we can "doe" about it!
Gotta protect that clock-out time!

726
00:43:17,820 --> 00:43:20,265
Raaaaahh!

727
00:43:24,782 --> 00:43:27,032
Heh heh heh heh.

728
00:43:29,465 --> 00:43:31,532
(Uyagawa) Skippity-skip-skip-skip~

729
00:43:32,173 --> 00:43:33,640
Whee-hee!

730
00:43:33,957 --> 00:43:35,907
Ugh, no way... I can't...

731
00:43:39,790 --> 00:43:42,273
(Uyagawa) Adios!

732
00:43:42,890 --> 00:43:44,840
Bye-bye~

733
00:43:44,915 --> 00:43:46,098
(Akane) Ahhh...

734
00:43:46,123 --> 00:43:47,198
(Uyagawa) Ha ha ha ha!

735
00:43:47,248 --> 00:43:48,564
(Siren sound)
(Akane) Huh?

736
00:43:48,848 --> 00:43:51,465
Akaneeee!

737
00:43:51,548 --> 00:43:52,614
Wha!?

738
00:43:53,765 --> 00:43:55,798
What is... going on?

739
00:43:57,148 --> 00:44:00,115
I thought about what I have to offer.

740
00:44:00,882 --> 00:44:02,648
Connections.

741
00:44:03,948 --> 00:44:05,382
Kyaaaa!

742
00:44:06,748 --> 00:44:08,700
Emergency vehicle coming through!

743
00:44:10,207 --> 00:44:14,656
(Singing) Momotaro-san, Momotaro-san...

744
00:44:29,115 --> 00:44:31,098
We're out of gas...!

745
00:44:36,120 --> 00:44:38,280
Kyotetsu Bus, please help!

746
00:44:39,140 --> 00:44:40,740
You got it! Leave it to me!

747
00:44:48,520 --> 00:44:53,780
Wait for meee! Leaving on tiiiime!

748
00:44:54,748 --> 00:44:56,798
...Exercise... no good.

749
00:44:59,615 --> 00:45:01,832
Akaneeee!

750
00:45:02,015 --> 00:45:06,932
When you're bad at something,
just rely on someone who's good at it!

751
00:45:08,970 --> 00:45:10,036
Oh, right.

752
00:45:10,061 --> 00:45:11,507
(Phone ringing)

753
00:45:14,040 --> 00:45:15,540
Grr!

754
00:45:19,457 --> 00:45:20,873
Grr!!

755
00:45:23,998 --> 00:45:24,998
Grr...

756
00:45:26,398 --> 00:45:28,132
Leave it to me, Akane!

757
00:45:35,107 --> 00:45:37,490
Raaaaaahhh!

758
00:45:39,557 --> 00:45:42,040
Raaaaaahhh!

759
00:45:43,973 --> 00:45:46,190
Raaaaaahhh!

760
00:45:46,580 --> 00:45:47,580
Go!

761
00:45:49,131 --> 00:45:52,281
Raaaaaahhhhhh!
(PA) The Tokyo-bound train is now departing.

762
00:45:52,482 --> 00:45:55,348
(PA) The doors are closing. Please stand clear.

763
00:45:57,032 --> 00:45:58,332
Huh!?

764
00:46:00,986 --> 00:46:02,906
Oh no!

765
00:46:03,173 --> 00:46:04,390
Bye-bye~

766
00:46:04,557 --> 00:46:07,423
Stop right theeeere!

767
00:46:08,823 --> 00:46:09,889
Huh?

768
00:46:12,248 --> 00:46:14,000
Railway police!

769
00:46:15,685 --> 00:46:19,731
Under arrest for Obstruction of Business
by Damaging a Computer—

770
00:46:19,898 --> 00:46:21,565
You're under arrest!

771
00:46:22,035 --> 00:46:23,776
Ow! Ow ow ow ow ow!

772
00:46:23,860 --> 00:46:26,100
4:59 PM. Arrested!

773
00:46:27,780 --> 00:46:30,020
I've always wanted to say that!

774
00:46:30,044 --> 00:46:32,170
Akane, did you hear!?

775
00:46:32,194 --> 00:46:35,853
(Phone) Like, a guy in a full bodysuit
was the culprit? Way too obvious.

776
00:46:35,894 --> 00:46:37,497
(Phone) First time I've seen one in real life.

777
00:46:38,506 --> 00:46:43,101
Well, in any case, case closed.

778
00:46:48,515 --> 00:46:52,948
Our obsession with the ideal of
"zero security incidents"

779
00:46:53,182 --> 00:46:58,365
left our emergency response capabilities
underdeveloped. We cannot deny that.

780
00:47:00,240 --> 00:47:05,573
We will promptly review our security response
and training systems,

781
00:47:05,890 --> 00:47:10,907
and establish a cross-departmental
dedicated security team.

782
00:47:15,947 --> 00:47:19,702
We deeply apologize to our passengers

783
00:47:19,727 --> 00:47:23,220
and all parties involved

784
00:47:23,245 --> 00:47:27,291
for the inconvenience and concern we have caused.

785
00:47:28,770 --> 00:47:30,490
We are truly sorry.

786
00:47:41,455 --> 00:47:42,510
Regarding this incident,

787
00:47:52,170 --> 00:47:56,090
their prompt disclosure of facts and investigation results
is commendable.

788
00:47:59,100 --> 00:48:03,430
So just deploying security products
isn't enough to be safe.

789
00:48:05,210 --> 00:48:09,930
Every individual — management and employees alike —
must have a sense of ownership.

790
00:48:11,506 --> 00:48:15,245
Assuming attacks will come,
whether internal or external,

791
00:48:15,269 --> 00:48:19,898
establishing incident response systems
and continuously reviewing them,

792
00:48:20,115 --> 00:48:24,482
in a way that fits the organization's culture —
that's what's needed.

793
00:48:26,248 --> 00:48:29,090
And stop hanging up phone calls!

794
00:48:33,090 --> 00:48:37,073
Cyberattacks on critical infrastructure
are expected to increase.

795
00:48:37,257 --> 00:48:42,340
All eyes are on Kyoki Railway's future initiatives.

796
00:48:48,180 --> 00:48:52,590
So Katsuragi got exiled. Serves him right.

797
00:48:54,690 --> 00:48:57,313
Still, it wasn't just his problem alone.

798
00:48:57,480 --> 00:48:59,956
In terms of a sense of ownership...

799
00:49:01,150 --> 00:49:02,150
Yeah.

800
00:49:02,740 --> 00:49:07,910
I never thought railway and information security
had so much in common.

801
00:49:09,930 --> 00:49:12,510
Hey, you've been thinking about something,
haven't you?

802
00:49:14,115 --> 00:49:15,282
Yes.

803
00:49:15,330 --> 00:49:19,836
The System Division was originally just parked
under PR for budget reasons,

804
00:49:19,860 --> 00:49:24,153
but I'm thinking we should make better use
of our position in PR.

805
00:49:44,539 --> 00:49:45,740
CSIRT?

806
00:49:45,910 --> 00:49:50,320
Yes, a cross-departmental team
that handles security incidents.

807
00:49:51,720 --> 00:49:54,060
Oh, what they announced at the press conference.

808
00:49:55,200 --> 00:49:57,140
We still have a long way to go, though.

809
00:49:58,250 --> 00:50:02,580
We need to earn trust so that reports
and communication flow to us naturally.

810
00:50:04,680 --> 00:50:08,461
Come to think of it, the key to solving this case

811
00:50:08,485 --> 00:50:10,320
was Agaho's tip-off.

812
00:50:11,160 --> 00:50:12,160
Well...

813
00:50:14,539 --> 00:50:17,061
In that sense, Agaho,

814
00:50:17,418 --> 00:50:20,272
thank you as always.

815
00:50:29,413 --> 00:50:31,580
Nice! Keep up the good work!

816
00:50:31,746 --> 00:50:33,135
And so, this time,

817
00:50:33,159 --> 00:50:36,746
we've decided to hire an ethical hacker
for the company.

818
00:50:37,013 --> 00:50:38,413
What!?

819
00:50:38,480 --> 00:50:40,613
(Ethical Hacker) Ethical~!

820
00:50:40,746 --> 00:50:44,160
Ha ha... It seems we still have
a long road ahead...

821
00:55:16,279 --> 00:55:19,346
The current year is Showa 99.

